Yes, exactly! Those are the three scenarios for sphere-OBB collision:

**1. Face collision**
- The sphere collides with one of the six flat faces of the box
- The closest point lies on a face (not on an edge or corner)
- One axis is clamped, the other two are within bounds

**2. Edge collision**
- The sphere collides with one of the twelve edges of the box
- The closest point lies on an edge
- Two axes are clamped, one is within bounds

**3. Corner (vertex) collision**
- The sphere collides with one of the eight corners of the box
- The closest point is exactly at a corner
- All three axes are clamped to their extents

**How to detect which scenario:**

After calculating the closest point, check how many axes were clamped:

```cpp
int clampedCount = 0;
if (abs(distance[0]) == obbHalfExtents.x) clampedCount++;
if (abs(distance[1]) == obbHalfExtents.y) clampedCount++;
if (abs(distance[2]) == obbHalfExtents.z) clampedCount++;

// clampedCount == 1: face collision
// clampedCount == 2: edge collision  
// clampedCount == 3: corner collision
```

This is useful if you need to calculate collision normals or response vectors differently based on the collision type.
